#!/bin/sh
# A stand-in `sprag` for the hook-command case: it only has to say IT RAN, by
# leaving a marker where the case can read it.
#
# The case links this file into a directory whose name contains a SPACE, which
# is the whole point -- a hook entry's `command` is handed to a shell, and a
# path a shell would re-split reaches nothing. `$0` is the path the kernel was
# handed, so the marker lands beside the case's own fixture directory without
# this file carrying an absolute path.
#
# TRACKED rather than written by the case: register item 467. A file any process
# holds open for writing cannot be executed, and this harness runs its cases on
# THREADS of one process -- a sibling forking to spawn a program inherits the
# write handle and holds it until its own exec.
printf ok > "$(dirname "$0")/../it-ran"
